dropped the body
--
N5008 [temp.arg.explicit]/9: "Template argument deduction can extend the
sequence of template arguments corresponding to a template parameter
pack, even when the sequence contains explicitly specified template
arguments" -- the standard's own example is `f<int*, float*>(0, 0, 0)'
with Types deduced as int*, float*, int.  The front end rejected
`count<int>(1, 2, 3)' ("found no match for symbol 'count'"): a variadic
template with explicit arguments was instantiated with exactly those, so
the pack was not extended by the call arguments.  When the call has more
arguments than the explicit ones account for, the explicit arguments are
now kept as a prefix for deduction.  The same for a pack followed by a defaulted parameter
(`template <class... Ts, int N = 7> int sum(Ts...)', [temp.param]/18;
`sum<int>(1, 2)').  Fully deduced calls work.  Noted as out of scope in
cpp14_function_template_pack_before_default_parameter (round 163b).
Header-free.  Fails on the round-163 build bin52.
